ns why p50 stays flat at 25ms.\n\nBut then I reconsider whether the statement path itself hides repeated full-history walks: purchases_this_month, plus biggest/smallest/mean each rescanning the whole history, alongside band and category breakdowns each making multiple passes over the month's purchases. That's roughly four full-history walks plus fifteen-plus passes over the current month, which could scale poorly for a shopper with tens of thousands of purchases \u2014 so I want to check if the deployed main has diverged from what I retrieved and search for a nested loop I might be missing.\n\nI did spot a real quadratic culprit in average_spend_per_item, which sums a running prefix inside an enumerate loop \u2014 that's pointless O(n\u00b2) work equivalent to just summing everything once, but I need to check whether that's actually the newly-enabled path or something separate from the caching behavior when the flag is off.\n\nTurning to the monthly statement panel itself, its rendering path never touches the cache and always walks the full history, so I'm checking whether that walk is linear or quadratic \u2014 it looks linear from what I traced, so the fault may instead be that the statement gets recomputed every request when it could be served from cache. But looking more carefully at typical_spend, it does n/2 iterations each calling remove-min which is itself O(n), giving genuine O(n\u00b2) behavior \u2014 that seems like the stronger candidate for the actual quadratic fault.\n\nI want to confirm which rollout flag actually gates this quadratic path, since the flag names may have shifted from what's in monthly_statement.py, so I should check the test files for the current flag naming before concluding.\n\n",
